- Critical: addLiquidity sets liquidityAdded = true before checking msg.value and block. If msg.value == 0 or block low, transaction reverts, but state changes revert too. So no issue. But it doesn't actually add tokens to liquidity? It only sets liquidityAdded, tradingEnable, liquidityProvider. Tokens are already in contract from constructor. ETH is sent to contract. Liquidity provider is msg.sender. removeLiquidity sends entire ETH balance to liquidityProvider, but does not remove tokens. After removeLiquidity, tradingEnable false. But tokens remain in contract? Actually sell swaps tokens for ETH from contract. removeLiquidity can remove all ETH after lock, leaving tokens in contract worthless. Liquidity provider can remove all ETH, rug pull. But lock prevents until block. However extendLiquidityLock can extend forever? Only liquidityProvider can extend, cannot shorten. So if LP malicious, can extend lock indefinitely. But can also remove after initial lock? If they extend, they can't remove before new lock. But they can wait and remove. Standard.

- Critical: receive() calls buy() internal. buy() uses msg.value. If someone sends ETH directly to contract, it buys tokens for sender. That's intended.

- Critical: transfer(to == address(this)) calls sell(value). But transfer returns bool. There is no allowance/transferFrom, no approve. This contract is not ERC20 compliant (no approve/allowance, transferFrom, events? It has event Transfer, but doesn't implement full ERC20). Users might think it's ERC20 but cannot transfer via contracts, no approve. But maybe intended.

